.TH AUDISP-ALERT "8" "November 2026" "Red Hat" "System Administration Utilities"
.SH NAME
audisp-alert \- real-time audit rule alerts aggregated by syscall
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B audisp-alert
[ \fIPRIORITY\fP ] [ \fBrules=\fP\fIFILE\fP ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBaudisp-alert\fP is an audit event dispatcher plugin. It reads string-format audit records from the dispatcher, selects \fISYSCALL\fP records produced by loaded audit rules, and emits syslog alerts. Counts are kept per syscall so repeated activity produces an aggregated count.
.PP
The plugin is compatible with the normal \fBaudispd\fP(8) plugin protocol. Set \fIformat = string\fP in \fIalert.conf\fP. The plugin does not change or acknowledge audit records and existing dispatcher plugins continue to receive the same events.
.PP
A rule file is optional. Each non-comment line has the form:
.PP
.RS
\fIsyscall\fP [ \fIkey\fP ] [ \fIthreshold\fP ]
.RE
.PP
The syscall may be a number, a name, or \fB*\fP. The key may be an audit rule key or \fB*\fP. When a threshold greater than one is supplied, an alert is emitted every \fIthreshold\fP occurrences for that syscall. Without a rule file, every \fISYSCALL\fP record is selected and alerts are emitted immediately.
.PP
Syslog priorities from \fBLOG_DEBUG\fP through \fBLOG_EMERG\fP and common facility names are accepted on the \fBargs\fP line. The default priority is \fBLOG_ALERT\fP.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following rule file selects an execve key immediately and batches openat alerts every fifty occurrences:
.PP
.RS
.nf
59 exec-watch 1
openat * 50
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.I /etc/audit/plugins.d/alert.conf
.br
.I /etc/audit/audisp-alert.rules
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR auditd (8),
.BR audispd (8),
.BR auditd-plugins (5),
.BR syslog (3).
